1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,667
This is STAT 331, video 2 b. It covers Lecture 1, slides 26 to 28: the chi-square, t, and F distributions.

2
00:00:11,117 --> 00:00:18,498
This video prepares you for two question types. Video 2 a had types A to D, so these are E and F.

3
00:00:18,948 --> 00:00:27,524
Type E: name the distribution of a statistic, and give its mean and variance. Lecture 3 does this for the t statistic.

4
00:00:27,974 --> 00:00:34,011
Type F: evaluate a chi-square, t or F density at a point, with the gamma function.

5
00:00:34,461 --> 00:00:50,739
The chi-square distribution. Slide 26 builds it from standard normals. Take three independent standard normal variables. One draw gives the values 0 point 8, minus 1 point 3, and 0 point 5.

6
00:00:51,189 --> 00:01:03,242
Square each value. The squares are 0 point 6 4, 1 point 6 9, and 0 point 2 5. The orange, purple and teal squares show them as areas.

7
00:01:03,692 --> 00:01:15,361
Add the squares. X equals 2 point 5 8. Each new draw gives a new X. The distribution of X is the chi-square distribution with 3 degrees of freedom.

8
00:01:15,811 --> 00:01:21,294
A square is never negative. So a chi-square variable is always 0 or more.

9
00:01:21,744 --> 00:01:36,379
In general: when Z 1 to Z n are I I D standard normal, the sum of their squares is chi-square with n degrees of freedom. The degrees of freedom nu is the number of squared standard normals in the sum.

10
00:01:36,829 --> 00:01:58,525
The chi-square density is 1 over 2 to the power nu over 2, times gamma of nu over 2, times x to the power nu over 2 minus 1, times e to the power minus x over 2. It is 0 for x below 0. The blue curve has nu equals 2. It starts at 0 point 5 and falls.

11
00:01:58,975 --> 00:02:05,545
With nu equals 3, the curve starts at 0, rises, and then falls. It is skewed to the right.

12
00:02:05,995 --> 00:02:10,497
With nu equals 5, the curve moves further right and becomes wider.

13
00:02:10,947 --> 00:02:18,349
The moments. The expected value is nu. The purple dots mark x equals 2, 3, and 5 on the curves.

14
00:02:18,799 --> 00:02:26,309
The variance is 2 nu. So a larger nu gives a wider curve. The variance is always two times the mean.

15
00:02:26,759 --> 00:02:40,263
Why is the mean of a chi-square with n degrees of freedom equal to n? Start with the variance formula of slide 13: the variance of Z is E of Z squared, minus E of Z, squared.

16
00:02:40,713 --> 00:02:49,310
For a standard normal, the variance is 1 and the mean is 0. So 1 equals E of Z squared minus 0 squared.

17
00:02:49,760 --> 00:02:51,787
So E of Z squared is 1.

18
00:02:52,237 --> 00:03:01,559
Now take the mean of X. By linearity, the mean of a sum is the sum of the means. The blocks show the n terms E of Z i squared.

19
00:03:02,009 --> 00:03:07,257
Each term is 1, by line 3. So the mean is n times 1, which is n.

20
00:03:07,707 --> 00:03:14,107
So the mean of a chi-square is its degrees of freedom. Question type E uses it in step 5.

21
00:03:14,557 --> 00:03:29,000
This part is optional, not on the slides. E of Z to the power 4 is 3. So the variance of Z squared is 3 minus 1, which is 2. The terms are independent, so the variances add: 2 n.

22
00:03:29,450 --> 00:03:41,503
Question type E: name the distribution of a statistic, and give its mean and variance. Lecture 3 does this for the t statistic. Method card E has five steps.

23
00:03:41,953 --> 00:03:49,676
Step 1: list the parts. Which parts are standard normal? Which parts are chi-square, and with how many degrees of freedom?

24
00:03:50,126 --> 00:03:55,950
Step 2: check that the parts are independent. All three forms need independent parts.

25
00:03:56,400 --> 00:04:11,056
Step 3: match the form to a slide. The table lists the three forms of slides 26 to 28. Row 1: a sum of squared standard normals is chi-square. Sections 2 and 3 build t and F.

26
00:04:11,506 --> 00:04:14,663
Step 4: read the degrees of freedom from the form.

27
00:04:15,113 --> 00:04:19,849
Step 5: give the moments from the slide, and check the conditions on nu.

28
00:04:20,299 --> 00:04:34,955
A type E question. Pause the video and try it. Z 1, Z 2 and Z 3 are I I D standard normal. X is the sum of their squares. Name the distribution of X, and give its mean and variance.

29
00:04:35,405 --> 00:04:41,976
Step 1 of card E: the parts are Z 1, Z 2 and Z 3. Each is standard normal.

30
00:04:42,426 --> 00:04:49,167
Step 2: I I D means independent and identically distributed. So the parts are independent.

31
00:04:49,617 --> 00:04:56,700
Step 3: X is a sum of squared standard normals. This is the chi-square form, row 1 of the table.

32
00:04:57,150 --> 00:05:04,681
Step 4: the degrees of freedom are the number of squares, 3. So X is chi-square with 3 degrees of freedom.

33
00:05:05,131 --> 00:05:07,712
Step 5: the mean is nu, which is 3.

34
00:05:08,162 --> 00:05:11,959
The variance is 2 nu, which is 2 times 3, or 6.

35
00:05:12,409 --> 00:05:20,260
Question type F: evaluate a chi-square, t or F density at a point. Method card F has four steps.

36
00:05:20,710 --> 00:05:24,081
Step 1: write the density formula of the slide.

37
00:05:24,531 --> 00:05:31,037
Step 2: put in the degrees of freedom and the point. The orange dot marks the point on the axis.

38
00:05:31,487 --> 00:05:49,301
Step 3: find the gamma values. Gamma of 1 is 1. Gamma of alpha plus 1 is alpha times gamma of alpha. Gamma of one half is the square root of pi. So gamma of 2 is 1, and gamma of three halves is 0 point 8 8 6 2.

39
00:05:49,751 --> 00:05:55,084
Step 4: simplify, and compute the number. It is the height of the curve at the point.

40
00:05:55,534 --> 00:06:08,419
A type F question. Pause the video and try it. Evaluate f of 2 for a chi-square with 2 degrees of freedom. Step 1 of card F: write the density of slide 26.

41
00:06:08,869 --> 00:06:12,965
Step 2: put nu equals 2 and x equals 2 into the formula.

42
00:06:13,415 --> 00:06:26,578
Simplify the exponents. 2 over 2 is 1, and 1 minus 1 is 0. So we get 1 over 2 gamma of 1, times 2 to the power 0, times e to the power minus 1.

43
00:06:27,028 --> 00:06:35,775
Step 3: gamma of 1 is 1, and 2 to the power 0 is 1. So f of 2 is e to the power minus 1, over 2.

44
00:06:36,225 --> 00:06:47,894
Step 4: e to the power minus 1 is 0 point 3 6 7 8 8. Over 2, this is 0 point 1 8 3 9: the height of the curve at 2.

45
00:06:48,344 --> 00:07:00,867
A second type F question. Pause the video and try it. Evaluate f of 1 for a chi-square with 3 degrees of freedom. Step 1: write the density of slide 26.

46
00:07:01,317 --> 00:07:06,735
Step 2: put nu equals 3 and x equals 1. We need gamma of three halves.

47
00:07:07,185 --> 00:07:19,665
Step 3: gamma of three halves is one half times gamma of one half. Gamma of one half is the square root of pi. So gamma of three halves is the square root of pi, over 2.

48
00:07:20,115 --> 00:07:22,569
Put it in. 1 to any power is 1.

49
00:07:23,019 --> 00:07:35,285
Step 4: 2 to the power three halves, divided by 2, is the square root of 2. So f of 1 is e to the power minus one half, over the square root of 2 pi.

50
00:07:35,735 --> 00:07:49,879
e to the power minus one half is 0 point 6 0 6 5 3. The square root of 2 pi is 2 point 5 0 6 6. So f of 1 is 0 point 2 4 2 0.

51
00:07:50,329 --> 00:07:58,649
The t distribution. Slide 27 builds it from two parts. The numerator is a standard normal variable Z.

52
00:07:59,099 --> 00:08:11,686
The denominator starts with a chi-square variable X with nu degrees of freedom. Divide X by its degrees of freedom, and take the square root. Z and X must be independent.

53
00:08:12,136 --> 00:08:24,339
Divide Z by the square root of X over nu. The result has a t distribution with nu degrees of freedom. Nu is the degrees of freedom of the chi-square in the denominator.

54
00:08:24,789 --> 00:08:38,186
From the chi-square moments, X over nu has mean 1 and variance 2 over nu. For a large nu, the denominator is close to 1. So t with a large nu is close to the standard normal.

55
00:08:38,636 --> 00:08:42,732
Compare the shapes. The black dashed curve is the standard normal.

56
00:08:43,182 --> 00:08:50,137
The orange curve is t with 1 degree of freedom. Its center is lower, and its tails are much thicker.

57
00:08:50,587 --> 00:08:59,376
The blue curve is t with 5 degrees of freedom. It is between the two. As nu increases, t comes near the standard normal.

58
00:08:59,826 --> 00:09:16,615
Zoom into the right tail, from 2 point 5 to 5. The t curves are above the normal curve there. A thicker tail means that extreme values are more likely. The denominator is random, and a small denominator gives a large ratio.

59
00:09:17,065 --> 00:09:26,303
The t density is symmetric about 0: f of y equals f of minus y, because y appears only as y squared.

60
00:09:26,753 --> 00:09:42,134
The moments of the t distribution. The mean is 0 when nu is larger than 1, by symmetry. When nu equals 1, the mean does not exist. The slide writes N A, not available: the tails are too thick for the integral.

61
00:09:42,584 --> 00:09:53,613
The variance is nu over nu minus 2, when nu is larger than 2. When nu is 1 or 2, the variance is infinite. The red region marks these cases.

62
00:09:54,063 --> 00:10:06,053
For nu larger than 2, nu over nu minus 2 is larger than 1. The blue dots show it. They fall toward 1, the variance of the standard normal, as nu increases.

63
00:10:06,503 --> 00:10:35,516
A type E question from Lecture 3, slide 31. Pause the video and try it. Z is beta 1 hat minus beta 1, over sigma over the square root of S x x. It is standard normal. V is the sum of the squared residuals over sigma squared. It is chi-square with n minus 2 degrees of freedom. Z and V are independent. Name the distribution of Z over the square root of V over n minus 2. For Tutorial 1, n is 7.

64
00:10:35,966 --> 00:10:44,073
Step 1 of card E: the parts. Z is standard normal. V is chi-square with n minus 2 degrees of freedom.

65
00:10:44,523 --> 00:10:48,491
Step 2: Lecture 3 states that Z and V are independent.

66
00:10:48,941 --> 00:10:57,623
Step 3: a standard normal over the square root of an independent chi-square over its degrees of freedom has the t form.

67
00:10:58,073 --> 00:11:06,884
Step 4: the degrees of freedom are those of V: n minus 2. So the ratio is t with n minus 2 degrees of freedom.

68
00:11:07,334 --> 00:11:16,059
Tutorial 1 has 7 months, so n minus 2 is 5. The ratio is t with 5 degrees of freedom: the blue curve.

69
00:11:16,509 --> 00:11:20,883
Step 5: nu is 5, larger than 1. So the mean is 0.

70
00:11:21,333 --> 00:11:31,338
Nu is also larger than 2. So the variance is 5 over 5 minus 2, which is 5 over 3, or 1 point 6 6 6 7.

71
00:11:31,788 --> 00:11:47,639
Tutorial 1, question 1 d, uses this t 5 distribution. Its 97 point 5 percent quantile is 2 point 5 7 1. The confidence interval uses it in place of 1 point 9 6.

72
00:11:48,089 --> 00:12:09,529
A type F question with the t density. Pause the video and try it. Evaluate f of 0 for t with 3 degrees of freedom. Step 1: write the density of slide 27. The denominator has gamma of nu over 2. The printed slide omits this gamma. With it, the total area is 1.

73
00:12:09,979 --> 00:12:19,088
Step 2: put nu equals 3 and y equals 0. Gamma of 4 over 2 is gamma of 2. The bracket is 1 plus 0.

74
00:12:19,538 --> 00:12:31,037
Step 3: gamma of 2 is 1 times gamma of 1, which is 1. Gamma of three halves is the square root of pi over 2. 1 to the power minus 2 is 1.

75
00:12:31,487 --> 00:12:43,348
Step 4: the square root of 3 pi times the square root of pi is the square root of 3, times pi. So f of 0 is 2 over the square root of 3 times pi.

76
00:12:43,798 --> 00:12:52,715
The square root of 3 times pi is 5 point 4 4 1 4. So f of 0 is 0 point 3 6 7 6.

77
00:12:53,165 --> 00:13:02,403
Compare with the standard normal peak, 0 point 3 9 8 9. The t peak is lower, because more area is in the tails.

78
00:13:02,853 --> 00:13:16,869
A practice question. Y has a t distribution with 1 degree of freedom. Evaluate f of 0, and give the mean of Y. Pause the video and try it. Step 1 of card F: the t density.

79
00:13:17,319 --> 00:13:30,801
Step 2: put nu equals 1 and y equals 0. Gamma of 1 over 2 plus 1 over 2 is gamma of 1. The denominator has the square root of pi, times gamma of one half.

80
00:13:31,251 --> 00:13:36,691
Step 3: gamma of 1 is 1. Gamma of one half is the square root of pi.

81
00:13:37,141 --> 00:13:45,653
Step 4: so f of 0 is 1 over pi, which is 0 point 3 1 8 3. It is lower than the normal peak.

82
00:13:46,103 --> 00:13:59,799
For the mean, use step 5 of card E. The mean exists only when nu is larger than 1. Here nu equals 1. So the mean of Y is not available. The tails are too thick for the mean to exist.

83
00:14:00,249 --> 00:14:07,737
This slide is optional. It is not on the Lecture 1 slides. It shows why the tails matter in Lecture 3.

84
00:14:08,187 --> 00:14:19,046
For the standard normal, 2 point 5 percent of the area is above 1 point 9 6, and 2 point 5 percent is below minus 1 point 9 6.

85
00:14:19,496 --> 00:14:34,088
For t with 8 degrees of freedom, the same tail areas start at 2 point 3 0 6. The tails are thicker, so the cut-off point is further out. Use the t quantile with the correct degrees of freedom.

86
00:14:34,538 --> 00:14:46,463
The notation t 0 point 9 7 5 comma 8 is the point with probability 0 point 9 7 5 below it, for t with 8 degrees of freedom.

87
00:14:46,913 --> 00:14:57,687
The last distribution is F. Slide 28 builds it from two independent chi-square variables. Divide each one by its own degrees of freedom.

88
00:14:58,137 --> 00:15:12,323
The ratio W is the first scaled chi-square over the second scaled chi-square. It has an F distribution with nu 1 and nu 2 degrees of freedom. The numerator degrees of freedom always come first.

89
00:15:12,773 --> 00:15:16,187
The blue curve is F with 2 and 2 degrees of freedom.

90
00:15:16,637 --> 00:15:22,034
The green curve is F with 5 and 10 degrees of freedom. It has a peak near 1.

91
00:15:22,484 --> 00:15:32,297
A ratio of two non-negative variables is never negative. So F takes only positive values. The slide gives no moments for F.

92
00:15:32,747 --> 00:15:46,059
A type F question with the F density. Pause the video and try it. Evaluate f of 1 for F with 2 and 2 degrees of freedom. Step 1: write the density of slide 28.

93
00:15:46,509 --> 00:16:03,533
Step 2: put nu 1 equals 2, nu 2 equals 2, and w equals 1. The gamma part is gamma of 2, over gamma of 1 times gamma of 1. In the bracket: 2 squared times 2 squared, times 1 over 4 to the power 4.

94
00:16:03,983 --> 00:16:14,309
Step 3: gamma of 2 and gamma of 1 are 1. 2 squared times 2 squared is 16, and 4 to the power 4 is 256.

95
00:16:14,759 --> 00:16:23,121
Step 4: the square root of 16 over 256 is 4 over 16, which is 0 point 2 5.

96
00:16:23,571 --> 00:16:41,043
This example is optional, not on the Lecture 1 slides. Pause the video and try it. Y is Z over the square root of X over nu, a t variable. Name the distribution of Y squared. Then check it with the brain data output of Lecture 3.

97
00:16:41,493 --> 00:16:48,683
Square Y. Y squared is Z squared over X over nu. Write Z squared as Z squared over 1.

98
00:16:49,133 --> 00:17:00,226
Step 1 of card E: Z squared is one squared standard normal, so it is chi-square with 1 degree of freedom. X is chi-square with nu degrees of freedom.

99
00:17:00,676 --> 00:17:06,009
Step 2: Z and X are independent, so Z squared and X are independent.

100
00:17:06,459 --> 00:17:12,817
Step 3: this is the F form, with X 1 equal to Z squared and nu 1 equal to 1.

101
00:17:13,267 --> 00:17:21,075
Step 4: the numerator degrees of freedom come first. So Y squared is F with 1 and nu degrees of freedom.

102
00:17:21,525 --> 00:17:36,607
Check with the brain data. The t value of x is 19 point 9 5 7, with 234 degrees of freedom. 19 point 9 5 7 squared is 398 point 2 8.

103
00:17:37,057 --> 00:17:51,052
The output prints the F statistic 398 point 3 on 1 and 234 degrees of freedom. It is the square of the t value. Lecture 11 uses F statistics.

104
00:17:51,502 --> 00:18:05,817
A practice question of type E. Z 1 to Z 4 are I I D standard normal. Name the distribution of three expressions, and give the mean and variance for parts a and b. Pause the video and try it.

105
00:18:06,267 --> 00:18:19,600
Part a. Steps 1 and 3 of card E: Z 2 squared plus Z 3 squared plus Z 4 squared is a sum of three I I D squared standard normals. This is the chi-square form.

106
00:18:20,050 --> 00:18:28,071
Steps 4 and 5: three squares, so chi-square with 3 degrees of freedom. The mean is 3, and the variance is 6.

107
00:18:28,521 --> 00:18:44,095
Part b. Steps 1 to 3: the numerator Z 1 is standard normal. The denominator is the square root of a chi-square 3 over 3. Z 1 is independent of Z 2 to Z 4. This is the t form.

108
00:18:44,545 --> 00:18:56,171
Steps 4 and 5: t with 3 degrees of freedom. The mean is 0, because 3 is larger than 1. The variance is 3 over 3 minus 2, which is 3.

109
00:18:56,621 --> 00:19:10,339
Part c. Steps 1 to 3: Z 1 squared is chi-square with 1 degree of freedom, over 1. The denominator is chi-square 3 over 3. They are independent. This is the F form.

110
00:19:10,789 --> 00:19:16,058
Step 4: F with 1 and 3 degrees of freedom. The numerator comes first.

111
00:19:16,508 --> 00:19:27,260
To recap. Card E names a distribution. List the parts, check that they are independent, match the form, read the degrees of freedom, and give the moments.

112
00:19:27,710 --> 00:19:43,710
The three forms: a sum of squared standard normals is chi-square. A standard normal over the square root of an independent chi-square over its df is t. A ratio of two independent scaled chi-squares is F.

113
00:19:44,160 --> 00:19:57,515
Card F evaluates a density: write it, put in the degrees of freedom and the point, use the gamma values, and compute. The next video starts the simple linear regression model in detail.
